export default "<p class=\"article-lead\">Here is a tale of two companies. Same industry. Same moment in time. Same clients, same geography, same macroeconomic headwinds. One is growing. The other is collapsing. The difference between them isn't talent. It isn't technology. It isn't budget or leadership quality or brand reputation. It's a decision that was made, or not made, a decade ago.</p>\n\n        <p>This week, I want to walk you through what that divergence actually looks like in numbers. Not projections. Not analyst predictions. Real revenue, real market cap, real headcount. Because the gap between the AI leaders and the AI laggards has stopped being theoretical.</p>\n\n        <p>It's now visible on every earnings call.</p>\n\n        <hr>\n\n        <h3>This Week in AI</h3>\n\n        <div class=\"ai-news-placeholder\">\n          <div class=\"ai-news-placeholder-icon\">\n            <svg width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"><path d=\"M13 2L3 14h9l-1 8 10-12h-9l1-8z\"/></svg>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"ai-news-placeholder-text\">\n            <strong>This Week in AI</strong>\n            This section updates with the latest AI news each week, developments translated into plain business impact for leaders.\n          </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <hr>\n\n        <p>Now for this week's deep dive...</p>\n\n        <h3>What's Actually Happening, The Numbers That Should Stop You Mid-Scroll</h3>\n\n        <p>Let me give you two numbers.</p>\n\n        <p><strong>Publicis Groupe: +5.6% organic growth. €14.5 billion in revenue.</strong></p>\n\n        <p><strong>WPP: -8.1% revenue decline. Market cap destroyed, from approximately £25 billion down to roughly £3 billion.</strong></p>\n\n        <p>These are two of the world's largest advertising holding companies. They compete in the same markets. They serve the same types of clients, global FMCG brands, automotive companies, consumer tech, financial services. They fight for the same talent. They face the same pressures.</p>\n\n        <p>The difference is this: <strong>Publicis started investing in AI infrastructure nearly a decade ago.</strong></p>\n\n        <p>They built Marcel, their internal AI platform that connects their entire global workforce, long before it was fashionable to have \"AI\" anywhere near your investor day presentation. They restructured, retrained, and rewired the entire organization around AI as a foundational capability, not a feature.</p>\n\n        <p>WPP is restructuring now. Reactively. In crisis mode.</p>\n\n        <p>The difference in outcomes isn't subtle. <strong>It's existential.</strong></p>\n\n        <p>And it's not just advertising.</p>\n\n        <p><strong>JPMorgan has told managers to stop hiring across multiple business units.</strong> A headcount reduction of more than 10% is underway. Goldman Sachs is actively limiting its growth in areas where AI is replacing junior analyst work. Across Wall Street, analysts estimate that more than 200,000 jobs are at risk.</p>\n\n        <p><strong>Amazon cut 30,000 corporate roles.</strong> Not warehouse workers. Corporate roles. Retail workforce cuts across the industry are up 123% year over year.</p>\n\n        <p>In the tech sector, Wisetech Global cut 30% of its staff. Their CEO said something I keep coming back to:</p>\n\n        <blockquote>\n          <p>\"The era of writing code is over.\"</p>\n        </blockquote>\n\n        <p>More than 141,000 technology industry jobs have been eliminated in 2025 alone.</p>\n\n        <p><strong>This is a structural realignment, happening across industries, in real time.</strong></p>\n\n        <hr>\n\n        <h3>The Analogy That Puts This in Perspective</h3>\n\n        <p>Let me take you back to 2005.</p>\n\n        <p>Physical retailers knew e-commerce was coming. Amazon existed. The data was there. The trend was obvious to anyone who wanted to see it.</p>\n\n        <p>Most retailers said some version of the same thing: <em>\"We'll figure out online. We've got time. Our customers still come to the store. Our brand is strong. We'll adapt when we need to.\"</em></p>\n\n        <p>They didn't deny the trend. They just believed they had more runway than they did.</p>\n\n        <p>By 2015, Borders was gone. Circuit City was gone. Blockbuster was already a cultural punchline. Hundreds of retailers were closing stores faster than they could renegotiate their leases.</p>\n\n        <p>The companies that went under weren't run by stupid people. They were run by smart people who kept finding reasons to delay the hard call.</p>\n\n        <p><strong>The compression from \"emerging trend\" to \"industry-defining force\" took about a decade. AI is compressing that same arc, from decade to years. Maybe less.</strong></p>\n\n        <hr>\n\n        <div class=\"monks-story\">\n          <span class=\"monks-story-label\">Monks Success Story</span>\n          <p>While legacy holding companies are losing market value and restructuring under duress, Monks has moved in the opposite direction. We actually reduced our workforce, from roughly 7,300 people down to around 6,500, and turned that into a competitive advantage. Not by doing less. Not by cutting corners. By doing more with a leaner, AI-augmented team that can deliver at a pace and price point that simply wasn't possible before.</p>\n          <p>The operating system behind that capability is <strong>Monks.Flow</strong>, our AI-powered production platform that spans the entire content lifecycle.</p>\n          <p><strong>PUMA:</strong> A fully AI-generated brand film, produced 2.8 times faster than conventional production timelines.</p>\n          <p><strong>GM:</strong> Over 100 distinct assets delivered in 18 days. That's the kind of output that used to take months.</p>\n          <p>Henry Cowling, our CEO, framed our philosophy in a way I think every business leader should consider: <strong>\"Our point of view has always been to lean in.\"</strong></p>\n          <p>Not \"wait and see.\" Not \"test and learn indefinitely while we maintain optionality.\" Not \"form a working group.\"</p>\n          <p><strong>Lean in.</strong></p>\n        </div>\n\n        <blockquote>\n          <p>\"The companies winning at AI didn't outspend their competitors. They out-decided them.\"</p>\n        </blockquote>\n\n        <hr>\n\n        <div class=\"action-callout\">\n          <div class=\"action-callout-label\">\n            <svg width=\"18\" height=\"18\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"><path d=\"M13 2L3 14h9l-1 8 10-12h-9l1-8z\"/></svg>\n            Your One Action This Week\n          </div>\n          <p>Pull out a blank sheet of paper. Draw two columns.</p>\n          <p>On the left: <strong>AI Leaders in your industry.</strong> Name them. What decisions have they made? What have they built? Where are they investing? What results are they reporting?</p>\n          <p>On the right: <strong>AI Laggards.</strong> Where are they still operating the old way? Where are they most vulnerable? Where is their margin being squeezed?</p>\n          <p>Now ask yourself the question that actually matters: <strong>Which column does your company belong in today?</strong></p>\n          <p>Not which column you want to be in. Not which column you're heading toward. Where you are, right now, honestly.</p>\n          <p>That's your starting point. And the only way to change which column you're in is the same thing that separated Publicis from WPP.</p>\n          <p><strong>A decision.</strong></p>\n        </div>";
